#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153050} :activity/id #uuid "888af819-3f31-5b7b-941a-2465f974729a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049133 :topic/id #uuid "dd88b5aa-842c-5755-bc2d-c71985927a3d" :topic/math-academy-id 2613} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151936 :question/id #uuid "260712d9-b48d-552e-a31a-0c2e74cafeb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6241"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345056}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "cf9d8974-1db3-5ee4-bd5f-2d355c9f3186" :step/math-academy-id 23446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151937 :question/id #uuid "07269d0a-faf3-5bf9-b397-8fc1574313a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6242"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345058}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "bd7ec3fb-24ee-52f0-a2ed-3ccf5bf49da7" :step/math-academy-id 23447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153349 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will describe a process for calculating a **lower bound** for the volume enclosed between a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane. \n\nAs an example, consider the rectangle $R$ in the $xy$-plane, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf7ef4752b0910b5544707eef97c854d8eeeedbc221a81c5fa631cf38b8b8d9a.png)\n\nNow consider the function $f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2,$ defined over $R,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34f7936b29e6a177ae0c831e36654dc5059514580a1947324a40d52320b83e5a.png)\n\nThe space underneath the surface and above the region $R$ defines a fixed volume $V.$ Our goal is to find a lower bound for $V.$ We do this by approximating this volume using rectangular solids. \n\nWe start by partitioning the $x$- and $y$-intervals of $R.$ Let's pick two subintervals of equal length for both $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/c3/c375b605ca6b7d7f2935218976f5e09db5ed2cfb64d811b1fe0a854d02646a36.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is now partitioned into $4$ rectangles:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nWe seek a *lower* bound for the volume $V.$ So, we let $m_{ij}$ be the *smallest* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We then define a rectangular solid in each subregion whose base is $R_{ij}$ and whose height is $m_{ij}.$ \n\nFor example, the rectangular solid corresponding to $R_{11}$ is given below. \n\n![](images/43/4324b984fb1056750f9bfceda58d57b2e0dbf1875ff21479d5ef93f088bfc50b.png)\n\nThe volume of this rectangular solid is\n\n$$\nm_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11})  = \\dfrac14m_{11}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the volumes of the rectangular solids formed by the partitions $R_{21}, R_{12},$ and $R_{22}$ respectively are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14m_{21}, \\qquad \\dfrac14m_{12}, \\qquad \\dfrac14m_{22}.\n$$\n\nDrawing all four rectangular solids, we get a volume that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/89/89d3706148d3f52096f94287c9cf4694b281bab18a8803bafe1b67d81e9b87fc.png)\n\nThe lower bound $L$ for our volume is the sum of the volumes of our four rectangular solids. This gives\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac14(m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12}+m_{22}).\n$$\n\nThe number $L$ is called the **lower Riemann sum with respect to the given partition**.\n\n**Note:** We don't yet know the values of $m_{ij},$ the height of each solid. We'll learn to figure these out a little later in the lesson. For now, we will just focus on writing $L$ as an expression of $m_{ij}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eda4b6f6-7ce7-5845-a288-86745288d9b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3953 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f6e14ef-cfe2-5f79-a589-ff562b89f766" :step/math-academy-id 23456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153182}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153181 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153416 :tutorial/content "Recall the region $R$ that we encountered earlier:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nEarlier, we saw that a lower bound for the volume $V$ of \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2\n$$\n\n \n\ndefined over the region $R$ with respect to the partition $P$ (shown above) is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac14(m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12}+m_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ over the subregion $R_{ij}.$ Our goal now is to calculate the values of $m_{ij}.$ \n\nWe start by considering $m_{11},$ so let's zoom in on $R_{11}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/04/042a236318a636de2fbfd9522dd221dbfd47906e63bc2290ca554bbcfccec958.png)\n\nNow, let's take a closer look at the function $f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that\n\n- as we *increase* $x,$ the value of $f$ *decreases* (because we're *subtracting* $x^2$), and\n\n- as we *increase* $y,$ the value of $f$ *decreases* (because we're *subtracting* $y^2$).\n\n![](images/4e/4e29408793329549172cdcc7113ec1d356d5b78e07b59b71e0d5284149375442.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in this subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of the subregion.\n\n![](images/13/13fb850ca6e17c9c68b29edd5b8f0a256a8099c9c1bf334270c5c90b4af98cc7.png)\n\nSince the function $f(x,y)$ decreases as $x$ and $y$ increase everywhere on $R,$ the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$ *always* occurs at the top right corner.\n\n![](images/92/9271ba6c0cb7d36b1480da239efb4896a715c3b0df244ebba7545486fe47dafd.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=2.5 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,\\frac{1}{2})=1.75 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(\\frac{1}{2},1)=1.75 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac14(2.5+1.75+1.75+1) = 1.75.\n$$\n\nRemember that $L$ is the *lower* Riemann sum, which is a *lower* bound for the volume between $z = f(x,y)$ and the region $R.$ Therefore, we conclude that the volume between $z = f(x,y)$ and the region $R$ must be greater than (or equal to) $1.75.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe50d6fe-4440-5c53-9c52-413256b1f114" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5775 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Minimum Value of the Function in Each Partition"} :step/id #uuid "7f3422a5-af38-55ce-9753-7b1b845c12a9" :step/math-academy-id 35472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153184}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153182 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152041 :question/id #uuid "60ec2df0-87b9-544f-b286-2ed5e17d92fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9476"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049878}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256742}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "1dfceb2d-adf2-5017-aaab-f12ee95af9ab" :step/math-academy-id 35473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153181}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153184 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153418 :tutorial/content "We need to introduce just a bit more notation. To illustrate, let's consider the region $R$ and the partition $P$ shown below.\n\n![](images/5d/5d5af7cb2f2b12a52bb624d12938aafd2d34b079ad5d28dc44133292b98956ec.png)\n\nIt's convenient to express our region $R$ and partition $P$ in a way that does not involve a diagram. We can do this using the Cartesian product.\n\nUsing the Cartesian product, we have $R = [1,4]\\times [1,3].$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- The partition of the $x$-interval $[1,4]$ can be expressed as $P_1 = \\{1,2,3,4\\}.$\n\n- The partition of the $y$-interval $[1,3]$ can be expressed as $P_2 = \\{1,3\\}.$\n\n- Therefore, we can write the partition as $P = P_1\\times P_2.$\n\nFinally, we write $L(f,P)$ for the lower Riemann sum of the function $f$ with respect to $P.$\n\n**Note:** We write $L(f,P)$ because the answer we get for the lower bound $L$ depends on the function $f$ and the partition $P$ that we choose. If we choose a different function or partition, then we'll get a different lower bound." :tutorial/id #uuid "445f66a1-3273-53e7-aba4-f3b3c5016df5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5781 :tutorial/title "Updating Our Notation"} :step/id #uuid "3fde6076-3c6b-56e6-8d2d-2d6c3868eb85" :step/math-academy-id 35506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153044}}] :activity/title "Approximating Volumes Using Lower Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152325}]}
